WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
- Oh, my gosh.

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.360
Nice to see you.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:03.193
- How are you?

00:00:03.193 --> 00:00:04.390
- I am so good.

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:05.223
- [Michael] You look amazing.

00:00:05.223 --> 00:00:06.056
- I know.

00:00:06.056 --> 00:00:07.040
Hey, at least we're not in virtual.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:07.873
- [Michael] Exactly.

00:00:07.873 --> 00:00:08.710
- And we have an audience.

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:09.543
- [Michael] Absolutely.

00:00:09.543 --> 00:00:11.357
- It's amazing.
- [Michael] How are you?

00:00:11.357 --> 00:00:12.407
- I know.

00:00:12.407 --> 00:00:13.260
- How are you?

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:14.093
- We're so good.

00:00:14.093 --> 00:00:16.640
I'm so happy that we have an
audience back, but congrats.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.030
So you became a doctor
since I last saw you?

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:19.863
Is this true?

00:00:19.863 --> 00:00:21.059
- Yes, yes I did.
- Okay.

00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:21.892
(crowd applauds)

00:00:21.892 --> 00:00:23.140
You should've brought like, glasses.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.170
- I am, I was... it's so funny.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.197
I got this honorary doctorate

00:00:29.197 --> 00:00:31.590
from speaking at Dillard university.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.820
So shout out to Dillard
university, I appreciate it.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.475
Thank you so much for the opportunity.

00:00:37.475 --> 00:00:40.530
It was a magical experience.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.550
Like I think there was
a lot of nerves in terms

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.007
of making a speech, obviously, but-

00:00:44.007 --> 00:00:45.190
- Oh, that would terrify me.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:46.370
- Yeah, it's terrifying.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.280
It is terrifying, but it
was like an opportunity

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.307
to kind of you know, help
these kids out and tell 'em

00:00:52.307 --> 00:00:54.730
you know, kind of what's awaiting them

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:56.210
now that they're done with school

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.700
and the best part was
meeting some of the graduates

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.393
who had to stop,

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.070
go have a baby, go-

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:05.903
- Life happened.

00:01:05.903 --> 00:01:08.150
- You know, life happened
and then they come back

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.990
and they finished and we all
know how hard it is to finish.

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.247
So all those people,
shout out to those people

00:01:13.247 --> 00:01:14.990
- Way to follow through.
- who really pushed through

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:16.480
to get it done, yeah.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.730
- Yeah, it's exciting probably
too, to just talk to them

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.617
and your whole future's
ahead of you, you know?

00:01:20.617 --> 00:01:21.910
What's an exciting thing.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:23.420
What did you study when
you were in college?

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.090
- I was an English major.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.370
- [Kelly] Oh, okay, wow.
- I was an English major

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:30.510
but that was kind of by
default because I... (laughs)

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:31.610
- That's by default?

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.660
- By default, yeah
because I went to college

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.203
as an architecture major.

00:01:38.847 --> 00:01:40.463
Yeah, there was a lot of math and science.

00:01:40.463 --> 00:01:42.320
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.280
- You were like, I am really into reading.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.904
- In architecture so I
had to kind of pivot.

00:01:46.904 --> 00:01:49.340
- [Kelly] Yeah. Architecture
is so cool though.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.480
- Architecture is amazing.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.650
- Especially when done so well,
especially go to like Europe

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.450
you see all these just old buildings

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:55.900
that have been there for years and years.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.520
Like, it's beautiful.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.130
- Yes, I fell deeply in love
with architecture as a kid

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.180
and then, you know, when I got to college

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.620
I realized I might not have
everything it takes to do it.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:09.160
So I had to pivot and I
was always pretty good

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.640
at reading and writing so I chose English

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.800
and it worked out for me.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.460
- That's the thing about
college that I think is fun.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.400
You go in thinking one thing

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.870
and then your whole, you
know, trajectory could change.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.440
You should read Pillars of the
Earth, it's by Ken Follett,

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.140
but anyway it's about
how they built, you know,

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.140
cathedrals right, but
those people that started

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.290
those cathedrals never saw
it to fruition obviously

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:28.830
'cause it took like
hundreds of years, right?

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.780
Anyway, a it's really cool
thing if you like building.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:31.792
- I do.

00:02:31.792 --> 00:02:33.980
- So do you still like building things?

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.620
Are you like, the guy that's handy

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.430
that does stuff around the house?

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.736
- Yeah. I... In my head, yeah.

00:02:39.736 --> 00:02:41.540
(audience laughs)
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:42.373
- That's different.

00:02:42.373 --> 00:02:45.140
- I am amazing in my head.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.233
- You're like Tim "the tool man" Taylor?

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:52.660
- I'm superior to Tim "the
tool man" Taylor in my head.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:53.493
(audience claps)

00:02:53.493 --> 00:02:57.460
I really fancy myself as the kind of guy

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.820
who can fix things and will,
you know, take that approach

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:04.393
and to my kids, if there's
a toy that's broken?

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.630
- You fix it, see?

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:09.385
- Dad can fix anything, anything.

00:03:09.385 --> 00:03:12.410
- Let's say I was interviewing
your wife right now.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:15.313
What do you think she would say? (laughs)

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.266
You're like, is she coming out?

00:03:19.266 --> 00:03:20.222
- You know how that happens, you know.

00:03:20.222 --> 00:03:21.280
You gotta make sure.
- No, no, no, no, no.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.640
I just bet she has a different story.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.910
She's like, he will try and
fix everything. (laughs)

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:29.400
- She will say what I... I
mean, she'll say, you know,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.773
he gets some of it done,

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:32.820
he gets some of it done
- [Kelly] (laughs) Love it.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.700
but most of the time
he has to call somebody

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.060
for some of the big stuff.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:38.078
- You know what though?

00:03:38.078 --> 00:03:38.982
- [Michael] The little things, I can-

00:03:38.982 --> 00:03:39.815
- That's a grown human,

00:03:39.815 --> 00:03:41.120
that knows when you need to call someone.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.776
- Yeah, and if you need a spider
swatted, I'm that guy too.

00:03:44.776 --> 00:03:45.863
- Yeah I can do that too, yeah.

00:03:45.863 --> 00:03:48.700
What's the coolest
thing you've ever built?

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.530
Or that you felt maybe not
the coolest, but you're like

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.573
this was really hard and I did it?

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:56.690
- So I'm a huge fan of Ikea furniture.

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:57.820
- [Kelly] Oh my.
(audience laughs)

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.274
- Now see, that is where
friendships can go wrong.

00:04:00.274 --> 00:04:01.800
Putting together that furniture,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.114
if you survive it, you will make it.

00:04:03.114 --> 00:04:05.960
- It makes you a stronger person, Kelly.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.260
It makes you a stronger person.

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:11.540
I feel like I have a degree in it now.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.100
I feel like I have an
honorary doctorate in it now.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:19.000
However, my crowning
achievement, I built my daughter

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.430
a dollhouse for Christmas,
I think a year ago.

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:26.950
Just over a year ago, like 2020.... 2020

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.840
and so at about 12 o'clock
at night, I started.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:34.410
- Oh no.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:35.350
- I didn't have a choice.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:37.450
Had to get 'em down, had
to wrap by the gifts.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:38.283
- [Kelly] Oh no.

00:04:38.283 --> 00:04:41.543
- 12 o'clock I started,
I wrapped around 4:30.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.534
But guys, the key was tequila.

00:04:46.534 --> 00:04:49.020
- I was gonna say, I was gonna
say what were you drinking?

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.454
(laughs)

00:04:50.454 --> 00:04:52.257
- Couple glasses of tequila.

00:04:52.257 --> 00:04:55.380
- Literally every parent, no
matter how much you prepare.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:56.490
I have everything almost done,

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.560
but there's always something
that happens like that night

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.390
and I'm like, how did this
slip through the cracks?

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:03.100
- How come this wasn't all done?

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:04.868
- Yeah, it's true.
- I should be going to bed.

00:05:04.868 --> 00:05:08.220
Yes, you would think you
would know that at that point,

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.140
I mean, she was maybe three
or four when that happened.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:15.590
But the joy that she got out
of that house was, I mean,

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:18.565
she still plays with it almost every day.

00:05:18.565 --> 00:05:19.398
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:05:19.398 --> 00:05:21.730
- And so that is my crowning achievement.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:24.830
It was a three, no four story plus attic.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.670
It's the biggest house I've ever seen.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.896
- I have built many a
playhouse, dollhouse things

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:31.940
with a friend that's really good at it

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:34.070
and we love each other so we survive it.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:37.176
Congrats on the series, I loved it.

00:05:37.176 --> 00:05:38.009
- Thank you, thank you.

00:05:38.009 --> 00:05:39.060
- I binge watched it.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.030
I kind of, I hated all of you the next day

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:43.320
'cause I had to be at work.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.640
I was actually interviewing Kristen

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.780
and I was like "I am so
tired because of you."

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.860
Like, because I couldn't stop.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.050
I was like, maybe I'll try.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.330
I'm a mom, you know, I got kids.

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:53.890
I'm like, I gotta go to bed,
put in bed, all these things

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:55.650
and I was like, okay I'll
watch a little bit of it,

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:56.960
as much as I can.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:58.764
I could not stop watching it.

00:05:58.764 --> 00:05:59.791
- Good.
It's so good.

00:05:59.791 --> 00:06:00.624
- Thank you.

00:06:00.624 --> 00:06:02.700
- It's funny but it's also
like, y'all are really good.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.130
So like I get that it's a satire,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.120
but it's also just like really good.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.020
Anyway, the glasses or the Clark Kent look

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:10.279
is a really good look for you.

00:06:10.279 --> 00:06:11.450
- Thank you, thank you.
- I just need to say.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:13.340
Yeah.
(crowd clapping)

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:17.180
Yeah, hey! (laughs)

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:18.540
You got a shout out from Jessie.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.890
No, but for those who haven't seen it,

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:24.490
first of all, don't start it
unless you wanna watch it all

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:25.510
because it's one of those.

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:26.740
You're not gonna be able to stop watching.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:28.020
It's not like a "I'll catch it next time."

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.490
You're gonna need to watch it all.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:30.670
But if for those who haven't seen it

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:31.830
tell everybody what it's about.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:36.830
- So it's a satire that
is basically satirizing

00:06:38.418 --> 00:06:42.890
the psychological thriller
that you keep seeing, you know.

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:45.310
Like The Mayor of East
Town and The Undoing

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:46.410
and all of those movies.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.860
So we're doing like the
Naked Gun version of that

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:54.000
and it is like... it is unbelievably funny

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.270
but so many people are confusing it

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.480
with a real psychological thriller.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.313
- [Kelly] That's true!

00:06:59.313 --> 00:07:02.500
- Because we intentionally
did this like whereby

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:05.925
we're playing it so dead
pan that you do care

00:07:05.925 --> 00:07:10.800
about who killed the
girl... in the window...

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:13.280
- Across the street.
- Across the street.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:14.810
- From the other girl in that window.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:16.995
- A wife in the house, or my ex-wife in...

00:07:16.995 --> 00:07:19.920
and so you find yourself really wanting

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:23.300
to know who did it, but
at the same time like,

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:27.550
the list of suspects is just,
I mean, one after the other

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:30.230
and my favorite is Tom Riley's character.

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:34.550
I mean, he just, he brought
out the... you know,

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:35.540
out of the trunk.
- [Kelly] It's so good.

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:38.410
It was like, I mean bawling laughing.

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:41.690
- Like I was laughing so hard
'cause it was so unbelievable.

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:43.270
When Kristen was here I asked if

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:44.650
she's spied on her neighbors.

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:46.530
So have you ever sped on your neighbors?

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:48.176
- Absolutely.
- You have?

00:07:48.176 --> 00:07:50.190
(crowd laughs)

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.730
- I don't think anybody...
there's no, come on.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:53.563
- You know what?

00:07:53.563 --> 00:07:55.640
I don't know if this is like...

00:07:55.640 --> 00:08:00.040
- If you live close enough to
somebody else or you've ever

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.470
lived in a major city and
you're like in a higher,

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:05.666
or you're in a hotel in
the middle of the city

00:08:05.666 --> 00:08:06.499
and you're looking out the window?

00:08:06.499 --> 00:08:08.500
- I love that you just told
us all the things you've done.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:11.400
- Exactly, these are all
the places I've spied.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:14.380
I've spied, I'm not ashamed
of it, we all do it, it's-

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:16.700
- No, here's the thing, am
I like, super egocentric?

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:18.115
'Cause I've never done that, I've never-

00:08:18.115 --> 00:08:18.948
- Come on!

00:08:18.948 --> 00:08:21.210
- No, I've never... I
don't know if I've cared.

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:22.043
I don't know if I've-

00:08:22.043 --> 00:08:22.930
- You don't have to care,

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:23.873
it's just the opportunity presents itself.

00:08:23.873 --> 00:08:25.900
- I think if I saw... (laughs)

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:27.410
(crowd laughs)

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:28.243
- Right?

00:08:29.305 --> 00:08:31.230
Or you'll just be in a hotel,

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:32.890
like you're high up in a hotel

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:35.380
and you look out the
window to see the view

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:37.070
and then you see somebody
walk across the window

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:38.523
and you're like, look away.

00:08:39.585 --> 00:08:40.575
(crowd laughs)

00:08:40.575 --> 00:08:41.930
Look away.

00:08:41.930 --> 00:08:42.808
You know what I mean?

00:08:42.808 --> 00:08:45.290
and you don't know, what did I just see?

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:47.130
- I don't think I've ever
had anything interesting.

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:50.500
- I can see right into
their bathroom, oh wow.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:52.010
I should probably close this.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:53.060
- [Kelly] I should probably... (laughs)

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:55.020
- I should probably close, room service?

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:56.187
- You know what though, if they're going

00:08:56.187 --> 00:08:58.500
and they're doing their
business with God and everyone

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:00.060
being able to see, that's their problem.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:03.310
- I mean, you know and let
me... I have seen some stuff.

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:05.000
(Kelly laughs)

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.970
- I've never seen anything, I
don't think I've ever noticed.

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:09.081
I don't know, I'm very focused.

00:09:09.081 --> 00:09:10.542
- Some people are
exhibitionists in that way,

00:09:10.542 --> 00:09:11.375
you know what I mean?

00:09:11.375 --> 00:09:12.883
- Oh, you know, I'm the
person y'all are looking at.

00:09:12.883 --> 00:09:15.546
(laughs)

00:09:15.546 --> 00:09:17.990
I'm just figuring out who I am, sorry.

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:19.329
- You're the one that gets spied on.

00:09:19.329 --> 00:09:20.162
- I'm the one that gets spied on,

00:09:20.162 --> 00:09:22.140
that's why I, yes, it's fine

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:25.640
Look, it's my house, if you're
watching that's your problem.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.140
I wanted to ask though about your kids,

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:30.520
so that your daughter is
giving you a music education?

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:31.410
Is this happening?

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:33.120
- [Michael] Yeah.
- How so?

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.683
- I'm weak, I'm so weak
for her, it's so bad.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:40.960
Like I taught her some old
school music that I grew up on

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:41.870
and I hoped that she would enjoy,

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:45.150
so I introduced her to like,
Candy Girl by New Edition.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:49.180
Right? And you know, the
